# Allowlist for the `Secret scanning` job in .github/workflows/platform-ci.yml.
#
# THE RULE: one entry per finding, each carrying the commit, what it is, and
# why it is allowed. Never a wildcard, never a whole path, never a rule id on
# its own. A blanket ignore makes the job green while checking nothing, which
# is worse than not having the job, because it also removes the incentive to
# look.
#
# Format is one gitleaks fingerprint per line:
#
#     <commit sha>:<file path>:<rule id>:<line number>
#
# Get the exact fingerprints from the job's own output. They are printed with
# the values redacted, so it is safe to copy them from the CI log.
#
# WHAT IS EXPECTED TO SHOW UP, AND WHAT TO DO ABOUT IT
#
# The archived BTP material under platform/_internal/archive (kubeconfig.yaml,
# xs-security.json, DEPLOY_SSO.md and a script with hardcoded SAP credentials)
# was tracked in git before it was archived. It is in history permanently, so
# the full-history scan WILL find its ancestors. Those are true positives and
# every credential they reference is considered compromised.
#
# The order of operations is not negotiable:
#
#   1. ROTATE the credential. This is the only real remediation, and it is the
#      part a scanner cannot do.
#   2. THEN record the finding here, with the commit and the note that it was
#      rotated on a given date.
#
# Never the other way round. An entry added before rotation hides a live
# credential and reads, to the next person, exactly like one that is safe.
#
# History is deliberately NOT rewritten. filter-repo and filter-branch break
# every clone and fork, and once the values are dead the rewrite is theatre.
#
# See platform/docs/runbooks/secret-scanning.md for the full procedure.

# ── Entries ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NOT A CREDENTIAL, so the rotate-first rule above does not apply here. Read
# this before assuming the entry hides something live.
#
# The finding is the INDEX_API_CONTRACTS constant in contracts_repository.py,
# and its value is the name of an OpenSearch index. The generic-api-key rule
# fires because the constant's name contains API and the value scores 3.55
# entropy, which is what any three-word hyphenated identifier scores. There is
# nothing to rotate and nothing was exposed: the value is a public identifier
# that also appears in the index mapping and in the migration script.
#
# The value is deliberately NOT quoted here. Quoting it verbatim is what made
# this comment itself a second finding on the first attempt.
#
# The line itself now carries a `gitleaks:allow` marker, which is the durable
# half of the fix: it travels with the line, so an edit that moves it does not
# raise the same false positive again. This fingerprint is the other half,
# needed because the pull request scan reads each commit's diff as it was
# written, and commit ffac9b3 predates the marker.
ffac9b3e7d14d7947059a10dd9ce792ba2153bc3:platform/packages/ask-admin-api/src/ask_admin_api/application/contracts_repository.py:generic-api-key:65
